      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d prefer this be a standalone function or class that fully encompasses the logic.  Something like this:\n\n```\nclass _SerialMatcher(object):\n  def __init__(self, serialname, devs):\n    \"\"\"Args:\n      serialname: None or str - device serial name/number\n      devs: iterable of (str, str) - USB (VID, PID) pairs\n    \"\"\"\n    self._serialname \u003d serialname\n    self._devs \u003d set(devs)\n\n  def match(self, device):\n    return (device.idVendor, device.idProduct) in self._devs and (\n        self._serialname is None or\n        self._serialname \u003d\u003d usb.util.get_string(device, device.iSerialNumber))\n\n\ndef get_usb_dev(...):\n  serial_matcher \u003d _SerialMatcher(serialname, map(_parse_vidpid_string, vidpid))\n  dev_g \u003d usb.core.find(find_all\u003dTrue, custom_match\u003dserial_matcher.Match)\n  ...\n```\n\nThinking through this more, I think all the functions in this module that are built around `get_usb_dev()` should really be grouped together in a public class.  I\u0027m okay leave that out of this CL, but I do think it would make sense for this module.\n\nI\u0027d probably have that class take `devs` as an initializer argument, while leaving `serialname` as an argument to each public method, but that\u0027s just my first pass thought and I haven\u0027t really prototyped that through.",
      "revId": "7151ac633115c0f26867e1bcec481559e45d2d81",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
